#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a94905c-48fe-5662-bb6c-2153f7725648" :knowledge-point/title "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of Two Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "a86fdc21-1c98-5929-8282-aed055b1b053" :question/math-academy-id "e-4745" :question/problem "If a machine at a factory takes $6$ hours to complete $2$ batches of refractory bricks, how many hours does it take to complete $10$ batches?" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of hours it takes the machine to complete $10$ batches of refractory bricks, we first find the unit rate of hours per batch and then multiply the unit rate by $10$ batches.\n\nTo find the unit rate of hours per batch, we divide the number of hours, $6$, by the number of batches, $2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\div 2 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $3$ hours per batch. To find the number of hours it takes the machine to complete $10$ batches, we multiply the unit rate by $10$ batches and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 10 &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the machine $30$ hours to complete $10$ batches."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea092786-a281-534e-803b-90e6c23b90a9" :knowledge-point/title "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of a Fraction and an Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "52c7bc18-eedc-504f-bbae-a501944834a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4746" :question/problem "At a certain sports center, there are $5$ equal-size pools. If a pump can empty $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ of a pool in $15$ minutes, then how many minutes will it take the pump to empty all $5$ pools?" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we can first find the unit rate of minutes per pool, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15$, by the number of pools,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\div \\frac{3}{2} &= 15 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15 \\cdot 2}{1 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{3} \\\\\n&= 30 \\div 3 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 5 &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d34e60c9-710b-50ca-a491-adc2b50e6edc" :knowledge-point/title "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of Two Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "4f61ec9c-9b87-5b25-9543-44b6043b7b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4747" :question/problem "Ava works in a coffee store. She earns a bonus of $\\dfrac 3 4$ of a dollar for each $\\dfrac 3 8$ liters of coffee sold. How much of a bonus will Ava earn if she sells $18$ liters of coffee?" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nby the number of liters,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4} \\div \\frac{3}{8} &= \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 8}{4 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{12} \\\\\n&= 24 \\div 12 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 18 &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee."}]}
